   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Matrices",
     "md": "A matrix is a rectangular array of numbers. Here a matrix is written row by row: [[2, 1], [3, 4]] has rows (2 1) and (3 4). Add or subtract matrices of the same size entry by entry. Multiply row of the first by column of the second: an $m\\times n$ matrix times an $n\\times p$ matrix gives $m\\times p$. Usually $AB\\neq BA$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Determinant and inverse",
     "md": "For $A=$ [[a, b], [c, d]]: $\\text{det}\\, A=ad-bc$. If $\\text{det}\\, A\\neq0$: $A^{-1}=\\frac{1}{ad-bc}$ [[d, −b], [−c, a]]. If $\\text{det}\\, A=0$ the matrix is **singular** (no inverse). $AA^{-1}=A^{-1}A=I$ (identity) and $(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$; $\\text{det}\\,(AB)=\\text{det}\\, A\\,\\text{det}\\, B$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Simultaneous equations",
     "md": "Write the system as $A\\vec{x}=\\vec{b}$. If $\\text{det}\\, A\\neq0$ then $\\vec{x}=A^{-1}\\vec{b}$ (multiply on the LEFT). If $\\text{det}\\, A=0$ there is no unique solution (none or infinitely many)."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Multiplying entry by entry: that is NOT matrix multiplication.\n- $(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$: the order reverses.\n- In the inverse, swap $a$ and $d$ and CHANGE the signs of $b$ and $c$, then divide by the determinant."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "$A=$ [[2, 1], [3, 4]] and $B=$ [[1, 0], [−1, 2]]. Find $AB$ and $BA$.",
     "steps": [
      "$AB$: row 1 of $A$ with columns of $B$: $2(1)+1(-1)=1$ and $2(0)+1(2)=2$. Row 2: $3(1)+4(-1)=-1$ and $3(0)+4(2)=8$. So $AB=$ [[1, 2], [−1, 8]].",
      "$BA$: $1(2)+0(3)=2$, $1(1)+0(4)=1$; $-1(2)+2(3)=4$, $-1(1)+2(4)=7$. So $BA=$ [[2, 1], [4, 7]], different from $AB$."
     ],
     "answer": "$AB=$ [[1, 2], [−1, 8]]; $BA=$ [[2, 1], [4, 7]]",
